Hi Hong Li,

First sorry for the delay in someone responding to your question.

Good news for the hedgehogs that you haven’t detected a decline 😊 Also, really neat that you have been able to quantify the shift associated with predator arrival.

Single session modeling density as a function of scaled distance to hedgerows (D ~ d2hedge) basically reflected this effect, i.e., the coefficient values of d2hedge gradually increased. I tried to incorporate this year interactive effect into multiple session modeling with the below code. With just two years, it worked but for some parameters the result is quite different with that from single session modeling ( each year modeled separately). So, could anyone please help me to check if my below code makes sense or totally not?

The difference probably comes from the fact that in the multisession model you assume detection is constant across sessions (years). To recover the single session model result using a multisession model, I’d add a session effect on detection (and perhaps include an interaction). You could also check the single session output and see which parameters look similar across sessions and which don’t – if there are difference in the detection estimates, that’s the variation you are constraining when not including session.

But for connectivity surface (I have already got the a2 value), I am struggling to find where are the detailed connectivity values? Anyone please could you let me know where to find the detailed connectivity output so I can get it plotted?

The connectivity surfaces are not produced in the predict.oSCR function. Unfortunately these surfaces need to be constructed ‘by hand’. You should be able to find code for doing this in the supplement of Sutherland et al. (2015) or Morin et al (2017).

Hope this helps. And please do reach out if I can be of any further assistance.

Chris

